! Bismuthene: a planar honeycomb monolayer of bismuth, with spin-orbit coupling.
!
! This is the P14 target system. Bismuthene is a two-dimensional topological
! insulator: the flat Bi honeycomb has a Dirac point at K, and the spin-orbit
! coupling of a 6p element -- which is large, bismuth being the heaviest stable
! element -- gaps it. Without spin-orbit the same calculation is a semimetal, so
! the gap here is not a correction to a number but the presence of a number at
! all, which makes it the sharpest possible demonstration of the feature.
!
! The geometry is the planar one bismuthene takes when grown on SiC(0001)
! (Reis et al., Science 357, 287 (2017)): a = 5.35 A, the SiC surface lattice
! constant, with the two Bi atoms coplanar. Freestanding bismuth would buckle;
! this is the substrate-stabilised structure without the substrate, which is the
! standard model for it.
!
! The cell is periodic in three dimensions, as a plane-wave code's always is, so
! the layer is repeated every 20 bohr (10.6 A) along z. That is enough vacuum
! for the bands near the gap; the deep 5d semicore states are flat regardless.
!
! conv_thr is far tighter than the answer needs so that both codes stop at the
! same fixed point and the energy terms can be compared digit by digit.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 4, celldm(1) = 10.109950, celldm(3) = 1.978248,
    nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 35.0, ecutrho = 280.0,
    noncolin = .true., lspinorb = .true.,
    starting_magnetization = 0.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.01,
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Bi 208.98 Bi.rel-pbe-dn-rrkjus_psl.1.0.0.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 Bi 0.333333333333 0.666666666667 0.0
 Bi 0.666666666667 0.333333333333 0.0
K_POINTS AUTOMATIC
 12 12 1 0 0 0
